# Heat and Thermodynamics: Temperature, Transfer, and Engines

> Heat is energy on the move; temperature says how hot a body is.
> This lesson covers scales, transfer modes, expansion, specific
> heat, gas laws, and the engines that turn heat into work.

## 1. Temperature versus Heat

| Aspect | Temperature | Heat |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Meaning | average kinetic energy of molecules | energy in transit due to a temperature difference |
| Unit | kelvin (K), °C | joule (J) |
| Measured by | thermometer | calorimeter |

## 2. Temperature Scales

@math label="eq:scales"
\frac{C}{5} = \frac{F - 32}{9}, \qquad K = C + 273.15
@/math

| Scale | Water freezes | Water boils | Notes |
| --- | --- | --- | --- |
| Celsius | 0 °C | 100 °C | everyday metric scale |
| Fahrenheit | 32 °F | 212 °F | still used in the USA |
| Kelvin | 273.15 K | 373.15 K | SI unit; cannot go below 0 K |

[note title="Absolute zero"]
0 K = −273.15 °C is **absolute zero** — molecular motion reaches
its minimum, and nothing can be colder.
[/note]

## 3. How Heat Travels

| Mode | Mechanism | Needs medium? | Example |
| --- | --- | --- | --- |
| Conduction | particle-to-particle contact | yes (solids best) | a steel spoon heating in tea |
| Convection | bulk movement of heated fluid | yes (fluids) | sea breeze, room cooler |
| Radiation | electromagnetic waves | no | warmth of the Sun in space |

Metals conduct best — free electrons carry the energy. The land
heats faster than the sea by day, so a **sea breeze** blows
onshore; at night the flow reverses into a **land breeze**.

@diagram tree id="heat-transfer-modes" layout="tree" aria="Three modes of heat transfer with examples"
  "Heat transfer" -> "Conduction: spoon in hot tea"
  "Heat transfer" -> "Convection: sea breeze"
  "Heat transfer" -> "Radiation: sunshine through space"
@/diagram

## 4. Thermal Expansion

Bodies expand on heating — length (**linear**, α), area
(**areal**, β ≈ 2α), volume (**volumetric**, γ ≈ 3α).

| Application | What happens |
| --- | --- |
| small gaps between rail segments | rails expand in summer heat |
| bimetallic strip in a thermostat | two metals bend differently and trip a switch |
| pendulum clocks in summer | longer rod → longer period → clock loses time |
| overhead power lines | strung slightly slack for hot days |

## 5. Specific Heat and Latent Heat

**Specific heat capacity** c is the heat needed to raise 1 kg by
1 K; water's ~4200 J/kg·K, the highest of common materials, makes
it a coolant and a climate stabiliser.

@chart bar title="Specific heat capacity of common materials (J/kg·K)"
labels:
Water
Aluminium
Iron
Copper
Lead

data:
4200
900
450
390
130
@/chart

**Latent heat** is the hidden heat of changing state: ice melts
at 336 kJ/kg and water boils away at 2260 kJ/kg while the
temperature holds still. A **calorimeter** applies heat lost =
heat gained.

## 6. Gas Laws and the Ideal Gas Equation

Boyle: PV = constant at fixed T; Charles: V ∝ T at fixed P.
Together they give the **ideal gas equation**:

@math label="eq:ideal-gas"
PV = nRT
@/math

with R = 8.314 J/mol·K — behind pressure cookers, tyres, balloons.

## 7. The First Law of Thermodynamics

@math label="eq:first-law"
\Delta U = Q - W
@/math

Heat Q supplied to a gas partly raises its internal energy ΔU
and partly leaves as work W done by the gas — the conservation of
energy applied to heat.

## 8. Heat Engines and Refrigerators

A **heat engine** absorbs heat from a hot reservoir, converts
part to work, and dumps the rest into a cold sink. Carnot proved
no engine can beat a limit set only by the two temperatures:

@math label="eq:carnot"
\eta = 1 - \frac{T_{c}}{T_{h}}
@/math

[example]
A steam plant running between 500 K and 300 K: η = 1 − 300/500
= 0.40 — at best 40% efficiency.
[/example]

The **second law** says no engine can turn heat entirely into
work (Kelvin–Planck), and heat never flows cold to hot on its
own (Clausius). A **refrigerator** beats that only by spending
work: coolant evaporating inside the box absorbs heat, and
condensing outside releases it.

## 9. Quick Revision

| Item | Remember |
| --- | --- |
| Absolute zero | 0 K = −273.15 °C |
| Water | c ≈ 4200 J/kg·K, highest of common materials |
| Ice melting | latent heat 336 kJ/kg at 0 °C |
| First law | ΔU = Q − W |
| Carnot | η = 1 − Tc/Th |
